TI  - Polarization in Geometric Opinion Dynamics
AU  - Gaitonde, Jason
AU  - Kleinberg, Jon
AU  - Tardos, Éva
PY  - 2021
T2  - Proceedings of the 22nd ACM Conference on Economics and Computation
AB  - In light of increasing recent attention to political polarization, understanding how polarization can arise poses an important theoretical question. While more classical models of opinion dynamics seem poorly equipped to study this phenomenon, a recent novel approach by H\ka zł a, Jin, Mossel, and Ramnarayan (HJMR) proposes a simple geometric model of opinion evolution that provably exhibits strong polarization in specialized cases. Moreover, polarization arises quite organically in their model: in each time step, each agent updates opinions according to their correlation/response with an issue drawn at random. However, their techniques do not seem to extend beyond a set of special cases they identify, which benefit from fragile symmetry or contractiveness assumptions, leaving open how general this phenomenon really is.
